module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_unit_arm_phantom_marker_takes_a_binder_name_in_the_value_namespace

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, Mitigatable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// Filed from the bare-name-resolution census that followed gunbc#15565 (DESIGN section 6b breadth,
// 2026-11-01): that census repaired one link -- the emitter deciding a LOCAL from the flat
// coproduct registry (v1.compiler.emit_rust effective_variant_parent, gunbc#15592) -- and this class
// is the other thing standing on the same arm, which no repair of the BINDING KIND reaches: the
// marker the emitter manufactures for a unit arm is a value item, and a binder is read as a value.
// It is filed apart from the v2 resolve-side class it is most often mistaken for
// (a_module_level_value_in_pattern_position_reads_as_a_binder); that difference is its own receipt.
data a_unit_arm_phantom_marker_takes_a_binder_name_in_the_value_namespace: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_unit_arm_phantom_marker_takes_a_binder_name_in_the_value_namespace" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "the Rust emitter names everything it derives from a coproduct arm's spelling -- the marker it manufactures and the unit-variant constants its prelude glob-imports -- against the binders of the module they are emitted into, so a spelling a parameter, a let or a match-bound field can take is never one a manufactured value item holds" } },

  receipts: [
    "**a unit arm's phantom ZST marker is a module-level VALUE item named with the arm's own spelling, and a local binder of that spelling is read as the item** (INVALID STATE: a coproduct arm carrying no payload owes a type-definite marker, so v1.compiler.emit_rust rust_phantom_zst_marker_def renders `pub struct ` plus the arm's spelling plus a semicolon into the module's item list. Nothing compares that manufactured name with the spellings binders in the same module take, and Rust has no namespace for bindings: a bare path in a pattern position that resolves to a unit struct is a pattern, not a binding.)",

    "HARM, MEASURED 2026-11-01 ON THIS BRANCH'S OWN BINARY, so this is not the bare-name registry read gunbc#15565 repaired -- it is the other half of the same arm, and the fix in gunbc#15592 does not reach it. A probe module declaring `type ProbeShadow = red | green` with a parameter and a let spelled `red` emits `pub enum ProbeShadow { red, green }`, then `pub struct red;` and `pub struct green;`, alongside `pub fn probe_capture_param(red: i64)` and `let red = 10;`. Cargo refuses the emitted policy unit with four E0308s -- two spans in each of the two functions -- the first reading, verbatim: error[E0308]: mismatched types: expected `i64`, found `red`, `red` is interpreted as a unit struct, not a new binding. The arm's spelling is the whole trigger, and no diagnostic names the coproduct: the author is told a binding has the wrong type, not that a manufactured item is standing in its name.",

    "WHAT IS ALREADY DECIDED, AND WHAT THIS CLASS THEREFORE DOES NOT CLAIM. Whether an arm owes a marker AT ALL is scope-decided, and was repaired as a separate class: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.phantom_marker_emitted_beside_a_same_named_type_definition records the marker decision asking what the arm's name BINDS in its own module's scope (v1.compiler.emit_rust phantom_marker_decision over v1.compiler.infer_emit_info type_decl_occurrence_binding: the module's own declaration, then a named import, then the kernel), owing a marker only on the scope's positive binds-nothing, and refusing on Undecided rather than emitting. That repairs the marker colliding with a DECLARATION. Nothing here asks that question again: this class is about the marker's NAME meeting a BINDER, where there is no declaration to find and so no shadow predicate to consult.",

    "THE NOT-YET-DECIDED HALF IS THE NAME, AND THE NAME IS WHAT RUST READS. phantom_zst_marker_owed_name hands out the arm's spelling and rust_phantom_zst_marker_def renders it verbatim; the only distance between that spelling and a binder's is the case of the first letter, because the emitter lower-cases a binder's authored first letter (`fn probe_binder_red(Red: Int)` emits `red: i64`, `let Red = 10` emits `let red = 10`) while a marker is emitted as authored. So an UpperCamel arm is kept apart from its binders only by the fact that no v1 source spells a binder with a capital first letter and keeps it, and a lowercase arm is one lint away from capture.",

    "TWO ROUTES PUT THE ARM'S SPELLING THERE, MEASURED APART SO A HALF-REPAIR CANNOT BE BOOKED AS THE CLIMB. The emitted file for the specimen holds the arm's name twice over: the phantom marker (`pub struct red;`), and the unit variant of the enum brought into module scope by the emitter's own prelude (`pub enum ProbeShadow { red, green }` with `use self::ProbeShadow::*;`). Four hand-written Rust cases, each the smallest thing that separates them, all under the same rustc: the marker alone refuses (E0308 at the binder); the glob-imported unit variants without any marker ALSO refuse (E0308, and the follow-on E0369 names the enum); the unit variants without the glob import compile; a PAYLOAD arm's name, glob-imported or not, compiles, because a struct-variant constructor is not a bare path. In v1 both routes are manufactured and both are keyed to the arm's spelling, and rustc names the unit struct in the message, which is what an explicit item outranking a glob import predicts -- the four cases are the measurement, the ranking is only the reading of them. A v1 source cannot take either route away: only the emitter decides them.",

    "RUNG: found at mitigatable, and nothing higher, because what keeps the harm out of the tree today is an unrelated lint plus a writing habit, not a rule of this emitter. The casing lints are WARNINGS by default: the same cargo run reported variant red should have an upper camel case name and type red should have an upper camel case name beside the four errors, and what turns those two into a refusal is RUSTFLAGS -- the emitted-closure host sets it to the repository's own denial row (src/v1/stage0/src/emitted_closure_compile_host.rs says so of itself at lines 47 to 50, taking gunbc.repo.repo_self_warning_policy repo_self_warning_denial_rustflags), and CI's toolchain step defaults it to deny-warnings. That row is not this emitter's rule, is per-invocation, and is already relaxed for one module in the very place that matters: `src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag` emits `#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]` as a module attribute for exactly one module, the error-primitives one, whose arms (DivideByZero, Overflow, Ok, Err) are today spelled in the single form the lints accept. Relax it, or lower the denial row, and a lowercase arm becomes legal source whose only meeting with a same-spelled binder is the E0308 above.",

    "MEASURED LATENCY, because `latent` has to be a number AND has to be counted in the right unit: what must be unreachable is an ARM SPELLING, not the marker an arm produces, so the denominators below are arms. Emitted side, every `pub enum` in the closures on this machine read with a brace matcher rather than a line grep: 911 enums over 3225 variants in the v2 compiler closure, 174 enums over 650 variants in the native-emission-controls closure, and ZERO lowercase-first variant names in either -- 3875 compiled arm spellings, none in the form that can capture. Source side, independent of what happened to be compiled: 1533 coproduct declarations over 2870 arms across `dag/` and `src/v2/`, again none spelled lowercase-first (a first screen returned 9 candidates; all 9 were `type X = fn(...) -> ...` aliases, excluded on inspection rather than counted silently). The only lowercase arm spellings ever seen in these closures are the pair `red` and `green` introduced by this class's own probe, together with the two markers those arms manufactured. So no arm in the corpus is spelled in a form that can reach a binder -- which is the difference between this row and its neighbour on the same arm: the neighbour was reached by a real source, this one has been reached only by a probe.",

    "FILED APART FROM THE v2 RESOLVE-SIDE CLASS, AND WHY ITS CEILING IS ITS OWN. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_module_level_value_in_pattern_position_reads_as_a_binder (gunbc#15125) is the v2 resolver READING a module-level value at a pattern atom as a binder-atom: a wrong decision over the graph, closed AtCeiling on the rung where v2.compiler.resolve type_decl_member_shape splits NotATypeDecl by Arrow and ConstantMemberEdge. Here nothing reads wrongly: v1's emitter PLACES a manufactured value item into the module, and rustc's own namespace rule does the reading, downstream of every decision v1 makes. Same spelling, opposite direction, different authority -- so this row does not rise with that class's AtCeiling, and that class's closure must not be read as covering this one.",

    "CEILING: mechanically preventable, climbable but unbuilt. The emitter already holds everything the decision needs in the one expression that renders the marker -- it asks the scope whether the arm owes a marker, so it can as easily ask the scope what binders the module can form -- which makes a rule that keeps a manufactured name out of binder reach a mechanical change, not a redesign of the boundary. THE CLIMB IS BOTH ROUTES OR IT IS NOT THE CLIMB: renaming or scoping the marker alone leaves the unit variant's glob-imported constant holding the same name (measured above), so this row is not climbed by a marker-side fix; the rung claim is about the emitter owning every spelling it derives from an arm. Not claimed structural: an underscore-led identifier is spellable by source authors, so a prefix excuses nothing unless the emitter owns the name outright and refuses the spelling as a binder.",

    "REVIEW TELL: a coproduct arm added or renamed in a module that also binds a variable of that spelling, with the failure arriving as a type error AT THE BINDER -- expected i64, found red -- and nothing in the diagnostic pointing at the coproduct, so the natural (wrong) repair is to rename the binder or to relax the lint. Any change widening `#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]` beyond the one module that carries it, or lowering the emitted-closure denial row, removes the only thing standing between a unit arm and a same-spelled binder and turns this class on for that module at once.",
  ],
  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "rust_phantom_zst_marker_def", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "phantom_marker_decision", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
